  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Wed May 13 2026\n\nUpper ridge exiting to the east and stronger southwest flow aloft\nwill flush out yesterday's moisture and lead to drier conditions \non Thursday. This will result in much less low cloud coverage, \nwith mostly high clouds left over. 994mb lee surface low centered \nover SE Colorado will extend a pressure gradient south across New \nMexico and Far West Texas. Breezy winds out of the southwest \nThursday afternoon, generally 15 to 25 mph with gusts up to 40 \nmph. Could see some localized blowing dust near the usual \nsensitive areas, but nothing widespread. Temperatures will be a \nfew degrees cooler as heights aloft decrease, but still 5 to 10 \ndegrees warmer than usual for mid-May with lowland highs in the \nlower-to-mid 90s.\n\nVery stagnant weather pattern Friday through the weekend which \nwill be consistently sunny, warm, and breezy each day. Longwave \ntrough pattern across the Western U.S. with the primary jet flow \nto our north will lead to mostly benign conditions outside of fire\nweather concerns. Sunday and Monday look to feature the strongest\nwinds of the period (20 to 30 mph), though still below Advisory \ncriteria. A subtle cool down in temperatures will also occur, with\nhighs right near normal Sunday into early next week (lowland \nhighs in the upper 80s. No precipitation expected over the next 7 \ndays.",
